WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.250
So today's lecture is
going to be on data.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.770
In the previous
lectures, up until now,

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.620
we've discussed how you
train a model, given data.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.420
So we've talked about
the architecture.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.860
We've talked about the
optimizer, tokenization,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.640
scaling laws, parallelism.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.130
That's all given
a fixed data set.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.450
And now, we're going to talk
about what data do we train on.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:30.740
So my hot take is that data
is the most important thing

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.729
in getting language
models right.

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:35.040
So [INAUDIBLE] might
disagree with this.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.590
He thinks scaling laws is
the most important thing.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.780
But here's my justification.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.270
Let's see what
companies actually

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.200
disclose in their papers.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.550
So if you think about all the
open-weight models, Llama 3

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.850
and even DeepSeek, they
obviously fully disclose

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.170
their architecture.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:56.150
And in the papers, they actually
talk a lot about the training

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.310
and how the training works.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.750
But basically, they don't
talk about the data.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.870
So if you look at the
Llama 3 paper, which

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.260
has a lot of details
about a lot of things,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.610
this is basically what
they say about their data.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.600
We create our data set from
a variety of data sources

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.520
containing knowledge
until the end of 2023.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.580
Now, to be fair,
they talk a bunch

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.370
about how they filter the
data, at least at a high level.

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:25.740
But obviously, this is not
really much information

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.250
about the data set.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:33.220
And there's some reasons
for this, secrecy.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.790
One is competitive dynamics.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:39.420
And the other is they
don't want to get sued more

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.145
than they already are, I guess.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.820
Before foundation
models, I think

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.240
data was clearly
recognized to be important

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.310
because you need
to annotate data

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.630
to drive supervised learning.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.910
Now, even though there's
less annotation involved,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.720
there's still the data
work, and it involves

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.500
a lot of curation and cleaning.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.780
So somehow, we
haven't moved much.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.170
Data is fundamentally this
kind of long tail of problem.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.380
And I think the
reason that people

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.630
think about it so much is that
it actually is very scalable.

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.300
If you think about
building a model that

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.040
does all different
types of things,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:27.520
you can easily hire a team
of several hundred people

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.540
who work on different
aspects of the data,

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.750
like multilinguality code.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:35.800
If you're multimodal, you can
do different types of images

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:36.710
and so on.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.542
Whereas architecture,
there's one architecture.

00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:41.750
You have a small team that
defines it, and that's it.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.680
Data is very parallelizable
if you think about how are you

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.060
going to allocate resources
in your language modeling

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.280
development team.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.310
So there's multiple
stages of training.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.410
So there's pre-training which
is the focus of majority

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.030
of this class.

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.590
And you train on raw data,
usually from the web.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:10.660
There's mid-training, which
is where you curate a smaller

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.280
set of high-quality data
documents aimed at targeting

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.070
particular capabilities, such
as math or code or long context.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.610
And then there's post-training,
where you fine tune

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.650
on instruction following
data or chat data,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.790
or you do reinforcement learning
to get the model to be actually

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.690
something that you can talk to.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.230
This is where typically things
like safety also fit in.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.540
But in practice, the
lines are blurry.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.040
And often in the more recent
models, there's more stages,

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.640
but one does not know
exactly what is there.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.098
But the basic idea,
I think, is clear.

00:03:46.098 --> 00:03:48.140
You start with large
amounts of low-quality data,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:53.410
and then you train on smaller
amounts of high-quality data

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.520
towards the end.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.660
Just a bit of terminology
that you've seen.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.978
So base model typically
refers to the checkpoint

00:04:00.978 --> 00:04:03.020
that you get after
pre-training and mid-training,

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.320
and then instruct models
are after post-training.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.180
So let's take an example
of what this looks like.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:12.080
So this is from AI2,
which has been releasing

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.440
a bunch of open-source models.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.480
So we know exactly
what's in the data set.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.200
So pre-training, this is a
typical pre-training data

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.100
mix at least for
open-source models.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.743
So there's some web pages from
this thing called DCLM-baseline,

00:04:28.743 --> 00:04:29.910
which I'll talk about later.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.950
There's code, academic papers.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.790
There's math and Wikipedia.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:39.620
And there's about 3.9
trillion tokens here.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.230
So now, if you look
at mid-training,

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.810
you see actually a bunch
of the same sources,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.250
but they're filtered down.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.370
So still DCLM-baseline
on baseline,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.680
but it's filtered down
from 3.7 trillion,

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:57.330
which was the majority of
that data set, to 700 billion.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.330
There's some flagged data
sets, which I'll mention later.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.040
Still, Wikipedia.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.140
We like Wikipedia, I guess.

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.910
And then there's
some new data sets

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.600
that are synthetically
generated and might as well toss

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.110
in the GSM8K training set.

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:11.800
Why not?

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:16.450
OK, so that's about 10
billion training tokens.

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.970
And then there's
a separate paper

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.210
called Tulu, which does
the actual post-training.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:24.700
And here's the various data mix.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:31.230
So there's basically chat
data from various sources

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.170
and a bunch of synthetically
generated data that

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:38.184
captures different aspects.

00:05:38.184 --> 00:05:42.510
So what are all these data sets?

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:45.660
How are they chosen
and processed?

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:51.180
So to set expectations and
not disappoint you later,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:54.020
there's not really
a good, I think,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.030
as you might imagine,
formalism or principle

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.030
for deciding these things.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.250
I think this is maybe
not that surprising,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.763
given the nature of this class.

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:06.430
Even for architectures,
we didn't really

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.580
have a good principle.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.490
But for data in
particular, I think

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.620
data is something
that's, I think, hard

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.690
to teach because what do
you mean by teaching data?

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.880
So basically, I'm going to talk
through the different data sets

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.130
that people have used over time.

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.920
I'll talk about where they come
from, some of their properties,

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:29.350
and hopes that you can use your
inductive powers to figure out

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:35.080
some of intuition for what makes
good data and what doesn't.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:41.680
OK, so I'm going to
start with pre-training.

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.980
And then I'm going to talk about
mid-training and post-training.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:50.110
But most of it, it's going
to be on pre-training.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:53.630
I'm going to start
way back in 2018.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:56.560
So this is the BERT
model, which some of you

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.950
might still remember.

00:06:59.950 --> 00:07:01.100
This is a big deal.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:07.100
So BERT was trained on
books and Wikipedia.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:11.530
So let's dive into what
that exactly means.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.910
I think the data sets
often not really,

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:17.680
I think, discussed
very much in--

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.810
because people look at
the model and the evals

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:20.870
and the capabilities.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.480
So there's this website
called Smashwords,

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:28.550
which came about in 2008, allows
anyone to go publish an e-book.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.350
So last year, there were
about 500,000 books.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:38.740
And so in 2015, there's this
actually vision language paper

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:41.590
that essentially scraped
Smashwords and created

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:45.460
this BooksCorpus, consisting
of self-published books that

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.980
were priced at zero.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.560
So they got 7,000 books.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:54.650
And this has since been
taken down because it just

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:56.310
violated the terms of service.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:59.490
So back in 2015, it
was the Wild West.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:03.020
People didn't think that AI--

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:08.540
and AI copyright wasn't really
much of a thing as it is now.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:12.680
So that's the BooksCorpus,
if you ever see that.

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:16.070
It's an old data
set, but it, I think,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:20.670
represents the importance
of books that has continued.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:22.170
Then there's Wikipedia.

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.140
Everyone knows Wikipedia.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:29.210
Just for fun, we can just
point at a random article.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.200
OK, sure.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.120
This is a random
article from Wikipedia.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.590
If you click again, you'll get
a different random article.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.930
OK, so here's a build random
building in Indonesia, I think.

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.870
It's been around
for over 20 years.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:50.640
And there's a lot of different
articles in different languages.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.240
I think it's important
to explicitly say

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.800
what's Wikipedia is.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.230
So it doesn't contain
any original thought.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.800
So everything is
coming from citations--

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:08.640
that's why there are citations
of actual, original primary

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:09.460
sources.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:11.670
So there's supposed to be
no opinions or personal web

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.500
pages or anything.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.710
And it's based on
Notability, which is--

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:21.040
it means that multiple
sources must have covered it.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.620
So I think this
already gives you

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.488
a kind of a sense of what's
in Wikipedia and what's not.

00:09:25.488 --> 00:09:27.280
Clearly, there's a lot
of valuable content,

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:29.740
maybe in the tails that
wouldn't be in Wikipedia.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:31.290
And there's a lot
of opinion that

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.460
might be useful that's
also not on Wikipedia.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:37.560
Recipes are not in
Wikipedia, and so on.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.450
So anyone can edit the content.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:48.160
But in practice, a
small number of people

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.550
contribute the majority.

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.950
So this guy had
five million edits.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.920
I think he probably
used some tool.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.200
So Wikipedia is this website.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.350
Now, every once in a while,
there's a dump that gets

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:03.130
produced.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:08.800
And you can go download some
zip file with all the Wikipedia

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:09.730
content.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.410
So just one aside
is that Wikipedia,

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.260
we think of as very
high-quality sources.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:19.930
Well, maybe reliable,
more reliable

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.750
than average internet article.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:26.690
But there's this thing that
everyone should know about,

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.380
which is relevant to
data, is data poisoning.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.490
So the idea is that this is--

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:38.140
Carlini has a series of
wonderful results showing

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:42.016
that everything is broken.

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:45.340
They show that you can
inject malicious edits right

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:46.640
before this periodic dump.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.770
So when the dump is coming.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:52.820
And so you inject this at it
so that it goes into the dump,

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:55.370
but before the edit
is rolled back.

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.070
So I thought it
was pretty clever.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:03.230
And we know that if you can
control the training data,

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.570
then you can basically
get the model

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:11.562
that's trained on such training
data to do various things.

00:11:11.562 --> 00:11:13.270
For example, ascribing
negative sentiment

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:16.010
to trigger phases
like an iPhone.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:19.750
So an adversary might be
able to leverage this process

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:23.960
and inject whatever they want
into something like Wikipedia,

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.770
even if you have
this rollback policy.

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:30.140
So I think since then,
this has been some patch.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.240
So I don't think you can
literally exploit this.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.050
But in general, I think
it's important to realize

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.900
that the data that
models are trained on

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.000
comes from the broad
internet, where

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.700
attackers and anyone
with various incentives

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.630
have actually quite
a bit of control

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:46.770
over the behavior of
the language model.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.960
And it's very hard to have
oversight into this process.

00:11:53.237 --> 00:11:55.070
That was a bit of a
digression, but BERT was

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:56.460
trained on books and Wikipedia.

00:11:56.460 --> 00:11:58.210
Obviously, back then,
people didn't really

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:03.680
care about data poisoning
for language models as much.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.420
And I think BERT seems
very old, but this

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:11.990
was kind of a big transition
between training on documents

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:15.890
rather than sentences, in
contrast to the billion word

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:19.040
benchmark that we
talked about last week.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:22.820
So that was 2019 or 2018.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:28.620
So GPT-2 collected a
data set called WebText.

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:32.330
And the idea here was that,
well, you have the web,

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:36.780
and it's kind of large and
probably of low quality.

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:40.680
How can we quickly get of
diverse high quality subset?

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.660
So the insight was that, well,
if you look at Reddit posts,

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.420
there's a bunch of
links that go out.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.010
And these posts can
get karma points.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:52.800
So why not take
the links that are

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.750
on posts with more than
three karma points.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:13:01.830
This resulted in a million
pages, 40 gigabytes of text.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.845
And that's what they
use to train GPT-2.

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:14.020
Besides the paper, they
didn't release the data set.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:19.110
So there has been since then,
open replication of WebText

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:23.225
that's often used in
language model research.

00:13:31.416 --> 00:13:35.730
So now, let's talk
about Common Crawl.

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:41.920
I think hopefully by the end
of this, whenever someone talks

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.247
to you and say,
well, language models

00:13:44.247 --> 00:13:46.330
are trained on the internet,
you can call them out

00:13:46.330 --> 00:13:48.620
and say, that's just false.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:51.310
And what does that even mean?

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:53.410
So let's talk about
Common Crawl, which

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:57.940
is maybe an academic
approximation of the internet.

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:00.380
So Common Crawl was
established in 2007.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:03.100
Every month, they
run a web crawl.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.470
So there's been about
100 different web

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:07.420
crawls over the last--

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:10.150
however many-- 17 years.

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:14.770
The crawl itself isn't actually
that expensive compared

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:16.630
to language modeling training.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:21.790
You can rent some
AWS machines and just

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:25.270
get it done in a
less than two weeks.

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:28.640
So the last crawl
was last month.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:34.040
And just to get a sense of
what this crawl looks like,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:37.270
so here are some statistics.

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:44.130
So there's about 2.7 billion
pages that were added.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:51.620
There's 100 crawls.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:56.270
Each crawl might have
slightly different web pages.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:59.350
But there's some overlap
because there's--

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:00.980
it's not clear what
the heuristics are,

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:03.740
but you might imagine that
sites that are rapidly changing.

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.680
They crawl multiple
times, and sites

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:07.940
that don't change very much,
they don't crawl as much.

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:11.620
And there's an explicit
attempt to diversify.

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:15.700
So crawling, just to very
briefly talk about this,

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.330
they use an open source library.

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:20.980
You start with a set of
seed URLs, which is actually

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:22.670
quite a large number.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:26.720
So it's not one website that
somehow you crawl the web.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:30.100
It's actually quite
hundreds of millions.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.370
And you basically
maintain a queue where

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:34.580
you have the crawl frontier.

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:36.122
And then you have
a bunch of machines

00:15:36.122 --> 00:15:41.610
that look at that frontier
and go crawl from there.

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:44.830
So basically, you're,
doing a BFS of the web,

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:48.090
but there's a lot of systems
dealing with the fact

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:51.840
that some sites might--
you have to be a little bit

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.790
careful about crawling.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:58.180
So there's questions of which
pages do you do you download.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.040
You have to respect robots.txt.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.060
You shouldn't
overload the server.

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:05.280
And if you've already
crawled a site, when do you

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:07.530
go back and crawl it again.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.450
And then there's a problem
that URLs are dynamic.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:11.980
So some URLs are very long.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.310
Multiple URLs might lead
to the same content which

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:18.930
leads to a lot of duplication.

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:25.780
So when Common Crawl crawls, it
produces data in two formats.

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.100
One is a work file.

00:16:27.100 --> 00:16:30.660
And this is the raw HTTP
response that you get,

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:35.140
which is often HTML
for HTML pages.

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:39.910
This does get converted into
text into a format called WET,

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:41.970
and this is obviously
a lossy process.

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:45.690
HTML to text loses information.

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:49.140
One note is that this
is not the only way.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:51.070
You can use their web
file, which is text,

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.490
or you can start with a
raw HTML, the work file,

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:55.390
and do it yourself.

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:57.630
And there's a few
tools out there.

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:02.950
In your assignment, you'll be
trying out different tools,

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:06.930
or at least doing the
HTML-to-text conversion

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:08.310
yourself.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.690
And this does make a difference.

00:17:10.690 --> 00:17:16.500
So the paper from DataComp-LM,
which I'll talk about a little

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:17.550
bit later--

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:21.930
this is ablation where you
look at different HTML-to-text

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:22.750
converters.

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:24.780
And using the raw
web files is actually

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:28.650
four points, a whole four points
lower than using Trafilatura,

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:29.375
for example.

00:17:32.290 --> 00:17:36.380
So there's some low
level details here.

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.690
One other thing I'll
say about Common Crawl

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:43.940
is that this is
deliberately not meant to be

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:47.370
comprehensive in terms of
crawling the entire internet.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:52.340
I think part of their policies
is to be gentle and polite.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:56.090
So, for example, not
all Wikipedia articles

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.210
are even in Common Crawl.

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.170
Maybe I'll pause
here in case people

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:08.945
have any questions
about data so far.

00:18:08.945 --> 00:18:09.445
Yeah.

00:18:09.445 --> 00:18:11.630
Does Common Crawl
do any filtering

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:16.610
on their own or sensitive,
offensive content?

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.620
So the question is,
does Common Crawl

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:23.450
do any filtering of its
own on sensitive content,

00:18:23.450 --> 00:18:26.544
offensive content?

00:18:26.544 --> 00:18:27.440
[INAUDIBLE]

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:31.670
Yeah, I think by default,
they're very permissive,

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.580
because idea of what
is offensive or not

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.140
is a fairly high-level
semantic decision.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:41.360
So there's definitely a lot
of offensive content in Common

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:44.540
Crawl and harmful content.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.260
There might be
kind of filtering.

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:49.430
I mean, there's some sites
which might be plain illegal

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:50.580
or something.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.490
There might be some blacklist.

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.390
I'm not sure about
the exact details.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:56.937
Yeah.

00:18:56.937 --> 00:18:59.270
Is there any way that they
can flag when they don't want

00:18:59.270 --> 00:19:01.380
to be included in the crawl?

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:04.370
Yeah, so the question
is, can a website

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:08.630
be flagged when they
don't want to be included?

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:10.650
And the answer is yes.

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:12.990
Well, yes, there is a way.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:17.240
So a website can include a
robots.txt file, which basically

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:21.210
has a bunch of rules saying
which crawlers they allow,

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:22.290
if any.

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.150
So if you look at
the robots.txt--

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:37.340
so this is-- robots.txt.

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:42.740
So, for example, New
York Times disallows--

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.390
for Googlebot, it
disallows a bunch of stuff.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.790
And then there's
different rules.

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:53.580
And you can see all your
favorite LM providers.

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:57.620
So it turns out
that many of the--

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:00.960
not all of these
are LM developers.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:05.720
But it turns out that most of
the frontier model providers

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:08.010
or developers have
their own crawlers.

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.770
Just because Common
Crawl is actually--

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.810
turns out to be quite
sparse in terms of coverage.

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.720
Even though it's quite
big, but the internet

00:20:14.720 --> 00:20:16.555
is a very big place.

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:27.500
But there's no formal way of
ensuring that robots.txt is--

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.190
it's kind of a guidance.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:35.940
So there might be folks that
are not respecting robots.txt.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:38.760
Yeah, over here, and then--

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:43.330
All this stuff [INAUDIBLE]
embedded media or images,

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:46.566
I guess those would be ignored.

00:20:46.566 --> 00:20:49.390
So the question is,
how are images handled?

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.510
So technically,
Common Crawl does--

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:54.295
it's just has a URL and
gets the raw response.

00:20:54.295 --> 00:20:55.920
So sometimes the
response will be text,

00:20:55.920 --> 00:20:57.940
and sometimes it will be images.

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:01.200
I think most of Common Crawl
it's biased towards text

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:03.270
because that's--
but occasionally,

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:06.270
you'll get other stuff.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:08.850
Of course, you could
develop crawlers

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:12.600
that explicitly go after media.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:13.680
Over there.

00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.420
Do you have any idea
of what fraction

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.400
of Common Crawl or
any other sources

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:19.800
are copyrighted material
that's horrible?

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.190
So the question is, what
fraction of Common Crawl

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.080
is copyright material?

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:27.750
I'm going to talk
about copyright later,

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:32.380
but I would say that
most of it's copyright.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.160
And that's a complex topic.

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:37.420
So I'll touch on
it briefly later.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:39.760
OK, let's move on.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.770
So Common Crawl is big.

00:21:42.770 --> 00:21:44.750
And I think even on the
first day of lecture,

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:47.290
I showed you that if you
just look at random samples

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:50.560
from Common Crawl,
it's really no good.

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:55.810
So there's been a lot of
attempts to filter Common Crawl.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:21:58.400
One of the earliest
attempts is called CCNet.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:01.480
This is from Meta.

00:22:01.480 --> 00:22:07.390
And the idea is that they
wanted a generic procedure that

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:12.905
could take Common Crawl and
return high-quality data sets.

00:22:12.905 --> 00:22:14.530
And in particular,
they were interested

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:17.140
in the multilingual coverage.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:19.840
So they had a bunch
of heuristics.

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.580
So they removed duplication.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.040
They ran language
identification,

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:28.320
which is basically
a linear classifier

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.930
to keep only examples
of a target language,

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:34.100
whether it be English or German.

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:37.980
And then, this is the key part
is that, to filter on quality,

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:43.160
they look at documents that look
like Wikipedia under a 5-gram

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:44.480
model.

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.260
So they take Wikipedia texts.

00:22:46.260 --> 00:22:48.710
They train an n gram
model, and then they

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:51.210
use that to score documents.

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:54.990
And the idea is that
Wikipedia, as you see,

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:59.310
has been used as a surrogate
for high-quality data.

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:03.470
And using that, you
can get more things

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:06.170
that look high quality,
where Wikipedia

00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:09.720
serves as a surrogate
for high quality.

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:11.930
And as we discussed,
Wikipedia obviously

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:15.140
doesn't cover everything.

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:18.930
So this is also not going
to cover everything.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:25.190
So they trained a bunch of
BERT models at the time.

00:23:25.190 --> 00:23:27.650
And they show that they
outperform only training

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.530
on Wikipedia.

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:34.430
And CCNet is a bit
confusing sometimes

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:36.590
because it refers to
both the tool, which

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:39.420
is a function of a
filtering function,

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:42.070
but also the data set that
they release from the paper.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:51.990
So meanwhile, Google was
doing some stuff as well.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:55.490
So they released this C4,
which stands for Colossal Clean

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:56.380
Crawled Corpus.

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:03.000
I guess the same insight that
you want to take Common Crawl,

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:06.650
you want to leverage
this large text somehow.

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:11.510
This paper, actually,
by Colin Raffel

00:24:11.510 --> 00:24:16.400
is more famous for
introducing the T5 model.

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:19.222
But it actually introduced
the C4 data set,

00:24:19.222 --> 00:24:20.430
which is a main contribution.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:23.300
It's a long paper.

00:24:23.300 --> 00:24:28.200
And the observation is that
Common Crawl, as we mentioned

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:30.690
earlier, doesn't have--

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:35.640
is most of it is not
useful in natural language.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:37.960
Let's say you start
with one snapshot.

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:40.510
So that's 1.4 trillion
tokens already.

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:44.520
They decided to use
just heuristics.

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:48.820
So they keep lines that
end in punctuation,

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:51.490
remove pages with fewer
than three sentences,

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:52.810
remove bad words.

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:54.820
You can click on this
to see the bad words.

00:24:54.820 --> 00:24:56.880
I'm not going to show that here.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:59.920
They removed brace,
which is interesting,

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:02.680
which clearly removes
a lot of code.

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:08.340
I guess Python might be kept
and some boilerplate text.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:11.370
And they only kept English.

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:12.970
They got a lot of
tokens out of that.

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:16.150
So it's kind of interesting.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:20.160
You see the trade-off
here is that whereas CCNet

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:23.250
used a model-based
approach to filtering

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:27.340
to make it look like Wikipedia,
this is an entirely rule based.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:29.170
So the advantage
here is that there

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:32.810
are sentences that don't
look like Wikipedia,

00:25:32.810 --> 00:25:34.570
but nonetheless are
well-formed sentences

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:37.030
that would end up in C4.

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:39.460
On the other hand,
there are sentences

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:43.690
that might be just very
spammy and also well-formed

00:25:43.690 --> 00:25:46.330
sentences that
might fall into C4.

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:49.270
So it's kind of
interesting that there's

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:51.680
this complementary nature.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:57.130
If you use model based, it's
only as good as your ability

00:25:57.130 --> 00:26:00.760
to curate positive examples
that are representative of what

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:01.790
you want.

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:06.140
And when you want a
very broad set of data,

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:11.150
it's often can be hard to get
that coverage because, well,

00:26:11.150 --> 00:26:14.770
that's the whole point you're
trying to get a lot of--

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:18.160
you're trying to curate
a diverse data set

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:21.130
in the first place.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:25.970
They also created a
WebText-like data set,

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:35.570
where they took pages
from OpenWebText links.

00:26:35.570 --> 00:26:39.270
Remember OpenWebText was
open reproduction of WebText,

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.870
which was used to train GPT-2.

00:26:41.870 --> 00:26:47.840
They looked at links from Reddit
posts with greater than 3 karma.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:53.070
Even if they use 12 dumps,
they only get 17gb of text.

00:26:53.070 --> 00:26:54.660
WebText was 40.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:58.400
So this suggests-- it gives you
a sense of Common Crawl is quite

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:01.220
incomplete, because you
took all of Common Crawl

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:05.060
and you apply the same filter,
and you got something that

00:27:05.060 --> 00:27:09.860
was about half as large as
WebText, which was basically

00:27:09.860 --> 00:27:12.950
doing its own crawl.

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:16.250
But nonetheless, this was useful
for improving a bunch of NLP

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:17.610
benchmarks at the time.

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:19.670
And if you look at--

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:24.735
now going back to C4, if you
look at what its composition is,

00:27:24.735 --> 00:27:28.800
you see that, well,
there's Wikipedia in there.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:33.590
There's a lot of patents
and news and so on.

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:38.560
All right.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.640
So we've talked about Common
Crawl and different ways

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:42.370
to filter.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:45.940
Now, let's talk about more--

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:48.820
now, we're entering
the GPT-3 era.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:52.140
There's a bunch
of models and data

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:57.700
sets, which will allow us to
get into some other ideas here.

00:27:57.700 --> 00:27:59.400
So GPT-3 data set.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:01.320
There's Common Crawl
which is processed,

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:06.480
WebText2, which is essentially
the same idea as what

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:08.020
they used for GPT-2.

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:14.250
This mysterious set of books
corpora, Books1 and Books2,

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:17.280
and Wikipedia.

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:23.010
So the result was I have about
400 billion tokens, which

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:26.790
by modern standards is actually
quite small, but at that time,

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:29.340
was quite impressive.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:34.680
So the Common Crawl processing
was they trained a quality

00:28:34.680 --> 00:28:38.970
classifier to distinguish
WebText, high-quality web

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:42.940
data, Wikipedia, and
books from the rest.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:46.440
So basically, the idea
of quality classification

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:49.120
is that you identify a
bunch of positive examples,

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:52.260
and then you try to look for
more stuff like that in a larger

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:52.810
pool.

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:55.600
So this is what they
determined to be high quality.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:58.115
And then they wanted
to get more of this.

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.400
So yeah, that's it.

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:09.910
So the Pile came shortly after.

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:14.610
So in particular EleutherAI
was this organization

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:18.820
that kind of bounced
up in reaction to GPT-3

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:20.660
and how closed everything was.

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:23.800
And they were trying to
reproduce open-source language

00:29:23.800 --> 00:29:25.840
models.

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:31.000
And this was largely a kind of
decentralized Discord-driven

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:34.210
volunteer effort where
everyone was just

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:38.240
like tossing in data that
they felt were high quality.

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:42.050
So they curated 22
high-quality domains.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:47.120
So you have some Common Crawl
OpenWebText, Stack Exchange,

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:50.230
Wikipedia, arXiv, and so on.

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:54.560
Here's some more statistics
about the general weight.

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:59.650
So this is still, if you
look at it, quite diverse.

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:02.480
And it's interesting
to think about--

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:05.230
technically, the
web, Common Crawl

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:10.070
could have most of this stuff,
assuming that you can crawl.

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:15.700
But often, you'll see
that people will go

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:19.190
and special case
different types.

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:22.100
For example, they
want to get more--

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:28.430
Wikipedia is handled
differently or mathematics

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:30.920
is handled differently.

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:33.780
So you have prior knowledge
about what's good data.

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:37.340
Then you can just go
out and use it directly.

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:44.240
So this was actually more data
than GPT-3 was trained on.

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:51.750
So they also noticed that
WARC was better than WET.

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:56.810
So they used this different
tool, just text to convert it.

00:30:56.810 --> 00:31:00.920
So there's a PubMed
central which was

00:31:00.920 --> 00:31:03.480
a lot of papers, which is nice.

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:09.680
So there's a mandate that
says NIH-funded work has

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:12.690
to be-- the papers
have to be open access.

00:31:12.690 --> 00:31:16.040
I think in AI, we
take it for granted

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:17.340
that things show up in arXiv.

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.110
But that's not true
for many other fields.

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:23.480
There's arXiv, of
course, and then there's

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:28.190
Enron emails, actually,
which is an old data

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:36.740
set which came out of a subpoena
after the whole Enron ship sank.

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:37.950
And why is this in there?

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.850
Well, it turns out
that it's really hard

00:31:40.850 --> 00:31:43.650
to come by email data
sets, as you might imagine,

00:31:43.650 --> 00:31:44.880
because emails are private.

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:47.100
So this is really the
best thing we have.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:51.140
So you might imagine there
might be some bias in terms

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:56.102
of the email knowledge
of this language model

00:31:56.102 --> 00:31:57.060
that's trained on that.

00:31:57.060 --> 00:32:00.850
But that's something
to think about.

00:32:00.850 --> 00:32:05.790
So just diving into some of
the different sources here.

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:11.480
So Project Gutenberg was
started a long time ago.

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:12.890
It's mostly English books.

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:15.430
Now, there's about 75,000 books.

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:20.340
And the biggest draw
of this is that these

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:22.590
are books that have
copyright clearance, which

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:26.100
mostly means that they're
in the public domain.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:29.410
I think 75 years have passed
since it was published.

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:32.340
So now, anyone
can use it freely.

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:34.080
But there's I think,
some books in there

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:36.100
that are technically not
in the public domain,

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:38.470
but it's OK to use.

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:43.830
There's a data set, PG-19, which
is books from Project Gutenberg.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:48.300
And this was a paper that was
trying to benchmark language

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:49.930
models on long contexts.

00:32:49.930 --> 00:32:52.920
So appealing thing
of books is that you

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:55.680
have really long
context compared to news

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:57.390
articles or even papers.

00:32:57.390 --> 00:32:58.110
Yeah.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:01.050
So based on what you
said in the data set,

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:04.230
is it possible that the
meteoric rise of xAI

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:08.850
is because they have access
to mining the tweets data

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:10.240
for training their model?

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:12.980
So let's say other
AI models can't,

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:15.850
which means that they have
access to better understanding

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:17.990
and nuance of spoken
human language more.

00:33:17.990 --> 00:33:20.120
And therefore, in the
long run, it's really--

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:24.130
Google and X are the only
big companies with access

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:27.220
to tons of tons of
human-generated data

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:28.910
with natural language.

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:31.640
Yeah, so I think the
general question is--

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:34.120
well, the narrow
question is, does X

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:36.620
have an advantage because
they have access to tweets?

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:39.680
And for example, any of
these big platforms--

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:42.490
Google has YouTube.

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:45.010
Meta has Facebook.

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:52.300
So there are restrictions on
what data companies can use,

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:55.750
even if they have the data.

00:33:55.750 --> 00:34:00.880
So it's not that literally the
Google can train on all of its--

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:02.480
like your Gmail or something.

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:05.140
I don't think that's the case.

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:09.730
That said, it is
true that companies

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:12.739
will have distinct advantages
and access to certain types

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:16.699
of data that might be public.

00:34:16.699 --> 00:34:22.679
So I think public doesn't mean
that anyone can train on it.

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:26.510
For example, YouTube is public,
but Google has special access

00:34:26.510 --> 00:34:27.530
to it.

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:30.139
So I think the
broader question is,

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:35.570
will companies that have access
to special data win essentially?

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:37.739
And by default,
the answer is yes,

00:34:37.739 --> 00:34:42.510
because I think that data
is the name of a game.

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:45.469
Now, interestingly,
obviously, Anthropic

00:34:45.469 --> 00:34:46.489
has a really good model.

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:50.060
And they don't have a
particular secret sauce

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:51.989
that I don't know of.

00:34:51.989 --> 00:34:57.800
So it is not everything,
but over time, I think--

00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:00.080
this is my opinion,
is that I think

00:35:00.080 --> 00:35:04.190
you'll see perhaps more
differentiation and more

00:35:04.190 --> 00:35:06.710
specialization as
companies leverage

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:08.155
the resources that they have.

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:12.930
OK, so that's Project Gutenberg.

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:19.590
Books3 was this project
that produced a lot of books

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:21.870
from this shadow library.

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:24.640
It contains books notably
from famous authors,

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:27.180
and since then has been
taken down due to copyright

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:28.210
infringement.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:31.620
So this was part of the Pile.

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:36.750
And so just a note
about shadow libraries.

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:39.210
There's a bunch of
different libraries

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:44.190
that basically disregard
copyright and bypass paywalls.

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:48.060
This is basically illegal.

00:35:48.060 --> 00:35:51.810
And there's been lots of
takedown orders and lawsuits

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:52.390
and so on.

00:35:52.390 --> 00:35:54.900
But usually, these
controls are circumvented

00:35:54.900 --> 00:35:59.010
because they just put them on
servers in different countries.

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:02.730
And the proponents
say that it makes free

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:04.840
what's really should be free.

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:08.140
But obviously, the law
thinks quite differently.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:13.180
In particular, LibGen
has four million books,

00:36:13.180 --> 00:36:16.480
which is a lot of books
compared to Project Gutenberg,

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:19.620
which has only 75,000 books.

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:24.280
And it has been revealed that
meta-trained models on LibGen,

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:24.970
for example.

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:27.305
And there's a big
lawsuit about it.

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.200
So moving on.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.800
Stack Exchange is a
collection of sites.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:38.620
Most prominently,
it's Stack Overflow,

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:42.480
which it started with, but has
grown to other areas like math

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.070
and literature.

00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:47.040
There's some
reputation and badges

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:50.100
to incentivize participation.

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:52.780
All of you probably have
used Stack Overflow,

00:36:52.780 --> 00:36:54.180
so I don't need to--

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:55.558
well, just for
fun, I like always

00:36:55.558 --> 00:36:56.683
looking at random examples.

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:09.290
This is a page that basically
gives you random Stack Exchange.

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:14.450
So I don't know if
these are any good.

00:37:14.450 --> 00:37:19.220
But here's a question,
and here's some answers.

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:21.243
So I guess this is
pretty familiar stuff.

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:30.620
So one thing to note is that
if you look at this data,

00:37:30.620 --> 00:37:35.680
it's really kind of looks
like a QA data set, which

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:39.910
is kind of what you expect
for instruction-following

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:41.960
capabilities and
real applications.

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:48.670
So I guess the lesson here is
that, by training on your web

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:51.670
data or a large-- in
pre-training, a lot of it

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:54.790
is just documents which
don't look anything

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:58.420
like what you would a user
would type into a chatbot.

00:37:58.420 --> 00:38:03.160
But there are subsets of
the pre-training data that

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:06.260
look remarkably similar
to what users would

00:38:06.260 --> 00:38:10.250
type coupled with the response.

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:13.160
And this is why the
lines are a little bit

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:15.500
blurry between what
is pre-training

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:18.542
and what's post-training.

00:38:18.542 --> 00:38:20.000
The cool thing
about Stack Exchange

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:23.090
is that there's also metadata
like comments and votes, which

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:25.070
can be used to filter.

00:38:25.070 --> 00:38:31.970
And the data dumps are provided,
although I think right now,

00:38:31.970 --> 00:38:33.200
this is--

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:36.780
if you use it
non-commercially, it's fine.

00:38:36.780 --> 00:38:39.560
But if you are a
commercial entity,

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:43.670
then you have to
pay for a license.

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:49.580
So in GitHub, which
everyone knows about.

00:38:49.580 --> 00:38:52.250
And this is, I think,
the primary means

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:55.915
in which you get code for
language model training.

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:05.180
And code is generally helpful
for programming, of course,

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:09.310
but it has also been,
I think, thought

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:12.920
to be helpful for reasoning
and other capabilities,

00:39:12.920 --> 00:39:16.360
although I don't know if
there's a paper that makes

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:17.480
that more rigorous.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:19.670
So here's a random GitHub.

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:21.440
OK, maybe this doesn't work.

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:22.540
Never mind.

00:39:22.540 --> 00:39:25.400
It used to work if you were
a random GitHub repository.

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:29.920
And the reason I'm doing this
is that I think the GitHub repos

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:32.950
that you visit and the
Wikipedia pages you visit

00:39:32.950 --> 00:39:36.050
are distinctly not a
representative sample.

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:38.620
And by sampling
randomly, it gives you

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:41.660
a sense of what's
actually in this data set.

00:39:41.660 --> 00:39:43.480
So when you look
at numbers like--

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:49.190
GitHub has however many
millions of repositories.

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:51.110
Not all repositories
are created equal.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:56.050
A random repo might disappoint.

00:39:56.050 --> 00:39:59.630
OK, so there's 28 million
public repositories.

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:02.360
And one thing that's
interesting is that GitHub--

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:03.400
what is a repository?

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:04.470
It's a directory.

00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:05.920
And some of it is code.

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:07.840
Not some of it's not code.

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:10.650
There's also notions of
issues and commit history

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:13.290
and all this other stuff.

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.090
And there's also a
lot of duplicates.

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:22.230
So in order to take GitHub,
which is all this raw data

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:24.990
and make it trainable
tokens, there's

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:27.210
actually a lot of
work that has to go in

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:29.945
to think about how you
want to best do that.

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:37.650
So GitHub Archive is this
snapshot of all the GitHub

00:40:37.650 --> 00:40:39.430
events that have happened.

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:44.670
And you can access it
using Google BigQuery.

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:50.760
So The Stack is a
project that fortunately

00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:55.870
produced this open-source
version of code based on GitHub.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:59.400
So they took all the repository
names from GitHub Archive

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:05.080
and Git cloned 137 repositories,
kept only permissively licensed

00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:13.010
ones and remove duplicates, and
the result was 3.1tb of code.

00:41:13.010 --> 00:41:15.320
So the nice thing about
code is that often,

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:18.130
but not always, the
license is made more clear

00:41:18.130 --> 00:41:21.820
compared to web pages, where
the license is almost never made

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:23.350
clear.

00:41:23.350 --> 00:41:26.788
So if you think
about this, this is--

00:41:26.788 --> 00:41:29.540
you see that there
is the live service.

00:41:29.540 --> 00:41:33.110
There's a website, GitHub,
that you go to every day.

00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:37.120
And then there's the snapshot
which gives you raw dump.

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:38.620
And then there's
the processing that

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:41.870
happens that turns it into
an actual trainable data set.

00:41:41.870 --> 00:41:45.410
So when someone comes to you
and says, I trained on GitHub,

00:41:45.410 --> 00:41:49.480
then you'll have to ask them,
what exactly does that mean?

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:54.220
What was the pre-processing
steps that were taken?

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:57.740
OK, so that was the Pile.

00:41:57.740 --> 00:42:00.740
Although I guess I took
a little bit of liberty

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:05.690
and digressed into the different
components of the Pile.

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:06.510
Moving on.

00:42:06.510 --> 00:42:08.520
So now, we're in 2021.

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:14.030
So DeepMind also
came on to the scene.

00:42:14.030 --> 00:42:16.580
The first large language
model they trained

00:42:16.580 --> 00:42:21.890
was Gopher on this massive
text data set which was--

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:25.880
the Gopher model actually
was not very, very good.

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:28.970
But the data set--

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:31.710
the paper does a great job
of describing the data set.

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:35.180
So MassiveText contains
MassiveWeb, which I'll

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:36.480
talk about a little bit later.

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:40.290
There's C4 and then books,
news, GitHub, Wikipedia.

00:42:40.290 --> 00:42:43.190
There's no details about
how those were processed.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:47.180
And as I mentioned before,
this is obviously--

00:42:47.180 --> 00:42:48.690
is not reproducible.

00:42:48.690 --> 00:42:49.370
It's fine.

00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:53.810
You train on GitHub, but how
exactly was the data processed?

00:42:53.810 --> 00:42:57.060
So MassiveWeb, you kept English.

00:42:57.060 --> 00:43:02.400
And here again, like CCNet,
they use quality filters that

00:43:02.400 --> 00:43:04.700
were based on manual rules.

00:43:04.700 --> 00:43:07.200
And they had rules which you'll
implement in your assignment

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:10.440
that look at things
like 80% of the words

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:14.700
has to contain at least
one alphabetic character.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:22.110
They also used Google SafeSearch
for toxicity filtering.

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:26.040
And I think in those days, one
of the main arguments for using

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.320
manual rules is
that you didn't want

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:32.130
it to be biased against
the model, because models--

00:43:32.130 --> 00:43:35.220
the only models that they
can run is very weak models.

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:37.660
And weak models don't
really understand the page.

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:42.960
And they're just going to have
probably pretty awful bias.

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:44.820
And also, there
was a consideration

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:49.380
that this type of
filtering can filter out

00:43:49.380 --> 00:43:52.110
kind of marginalized data from
marginalized groups that didn't

00:43:52.110 --> 00:43:56.340
look exactly like Wikipedia.

00:43:56.340 --> 00:43:59.230
But as you see later,
this has flipped.

00:43:59.230 --> 00:44:03.160
And now, everyone's doing
model-based filtering.

00:44:03.160 --> 00:44:06.420
So their data set
was 10 terabytes

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:12.630
of text, which is, I
guess, maybe let's say four

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:15.550
or five trillion tokens,
just an estimate.

00:44:15.550 --> 00:44:18.400
Although Gopher was only
trained on 300 billion,

00:44:18.400 --> 00:44:20.950
which is not very many tokens.

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:24.210
I think that's around
the same number of GPT-3.

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:30.090
So in 2022, we have Llama.

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:37.860
So the data set for Llama was
Common Crawl process with CCNet.

00:44:37.860 --> 00:44:41.100
And here are the
classifier here.

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:42.100
There's a subtlety here.

00:44:42.100 --> 00:44:44.400
Remember GPT-3 was
classified whether it looked

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:47.820
like a Wikipedia page or not.

00:44:47.820 --> 00:44:50.160
Llama was trained
on classifier which

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:53.550
predicted do you look like a
page that was referenced out

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:55.180
of Wikipedia or not.

00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:57.580
And I guess the idea
is that Wikipedia

00:44:57.580 --> 00:45:00.350
will cite high-quality pages.

00:45:00.350 --> 00:45:02.140
And most of those
pages might not

00:45:02.140 --> 00:45:04.420
look like a Wikipedia
article, but nonetheless are

00:45:04.420 --> 00:45:05.300
high quality.

00:45:05.300 --> 00:45:10.990
So again, this link structure
which we saw in the GPT-2

00:45:10.990 --> 00:45:14.890
WebText is showing up here.

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:17.230
They also included C4.

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:17.840
Why not?

00:45:17.840 --> 00:45:20.530
They use GitHub.

00:45:20.530 --> 00:45:22.570
They kept permissive
licenses filtered

00:45:22.570 --> 00:45:26.980
based on some manual rules,
Wikipedia, Project Gutenberg,

00:45:26.980 --> 00:45:30.910
and Books3, which got
them in a lot of trouble,

00:45:30.910 --> 00:45:34.810
and arXiv, Stack Exchange.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:38.710
So they got 1.2 trillion tokens.

00:45:38.710 --> 00:45:40.400
So they didn't
release the data set,

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:46.720
but together reproduced this
data set and something called

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.000
RedPajama, which
now you can go and--

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:51.880
you have the data processing
code and the data,

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:54.720
which you can take out.

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:56.070
So this was a reproduction.

00:45:56.070 --> 00:45:58.050
So this was clearly not optimal.

00:45:58.050 --> 00:46:03.020
And Cerebus did further
deduplication and ended up with

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:07.230
a 627-billion parameter subset.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:11.480
There's also RedPajama-V2,
which is a little bit confusing

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:13.320
because this is something else.

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:18.410
This is essentially taking
Common Crawl snapshots

00:46:18.410 --> 00:46:20.720
and producing 30 trillion
tokens with all sorts

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:23.090
of different quality signals.

00:46:23.090 --> 00:46:25.820
So this is a resource
for doing research

00:46:25.820 --> 00:46:29.300
on how to filter based on
the quality signals that

00:46:29.300 --> 00:46:31.400
are computed.

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:34.220
OK, so that was Llama.

00:46:34.220 --> 00:46:39.270
And then the RefinedWeb
was another paper.

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:41.240
And here, the thesis is--

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:43.508
well, remember how
we saw the Pile.

00:46:43.508 --> 00:46:45.800
There was web data, and there
was all this other stuff.

00:46:45.800 --> 00:46:47.630
And their point was
like, well, maybe

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:50.900
if we do a good enough job
filtering the web data, that's

00:46:50.900 --> 00:46:54.390
all you need, because
technically, the internet has

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:55.660
everything in some sense.

00:46:55.660 --> 00:46:58.710
If you think about it, if you
can access it via computer

00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:00.460
and it's connected
on the internet,

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:02.210
then maybe that's good enough.

00:47:04.920 --> 00:47:11.460
So the RefinedWeb-- let's see
if we can look at some examples

00:47:11.460 --> 00:47:13.260
here.

00:47:13.260 --> 00:47:18.840
The data is on Hugging Face,
and it kind of looks like this.

00:47:21.670 --> 00:47:25.690
The resolution is probably
not large enough to--

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:29.820
OK, anyway, scrap that.

00:47:29.820 --> 00:47:33.000
They used Trafilatura
for extracting content

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:37.050
because as we noted, Trafilatura
is better than just using

00:47:37.050 --> 00:47:40.710
the web files that
Common Crawl provides.

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:42.510
They used Gopher rules.

00:47:42.510 --> 00:47:44.310
And they made a
point of we're going

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:49.290
to avoid ML-based
filtering to avoid biases.

00:47:49.290 --> 00:47:53.780
And then they did some
fuzzy duplication.

00:47:53.780 --> 00:47:58.160
So they had a 5 trillion token
data set, which is quite large,

00:47:58.160 --> 00:48:02.620
but they only released
600 billion of it.

00:48:02.620 --> 00:48:05.170
FineWeb from Hugging
Face was started

00:48:05.170 --> 00:48:10.910
as a replication of Refined-Web,
but they tried to improve it.

00:48:10.910 --> 00:48:13.210
So they used all the
Common Crawl dumps

00:48:13.210 --> 00:48:16.220
I think at the time,
did some filtering.

00:48:16.220 --> 00:48:18.920
Again, this is still
using manual rules,

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:21.520
no model-based filtering.

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:24.310
And they did some
deduplication and did

00:48:24.310 --> 00:48:26.690
some basic anonymization.

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:29.990
So they got 15 trillion
tokens out of this.

00:48:29.990 --> 00:48:33.790
So this is still, I
think, a really nice data

00:48:33.790 --> 00:48:35.370
set because it--

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:39.110
dealing with Common Crawl
is going to be a pain,

00:48:39.110 --> 00:48:40.510
but this is a--

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:45.370
I would consider FineWeb
as a lightly filtered data

00:48:45.370 --> 00:48:48.930
set that you can further do
model-based filtering on.

00:48:52.120 --> 00:49:01.660
So jumping ahead, so AI2 has a
series of models called OLMo.

00:49:01.660 --> 00:49:07.930
Their initial model was
trained on the Dolma data set.

00:49:07.930 --> 00:49:11.090
And this is a composition.

00:49:11.090 --> 00:49:12.303
So we have Common Crawl.

00:49:12.303 --> 00:49:13.720
We have The Stack,
which we talked

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:17.500
about, which has code, C4,
which you know, Reddit.

00:49:20.260 --> 00:49:24.080
A2 has Semantic Scholar.

00:49:24.080 --> 00:49:26.410
So I think this is
derived from that,

00:49:26.410 --> 00:49:30.760
Project Gutenberg and
Wikipedia and you know about.

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:38.890
So the Reddit comes
from this project,

00:49:38.890 --> 00:49:42.860
but they include the submissions
and the comments separately.

00:49:42.860 --> 00:49:44.540
So you don't have
this thread structure.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:45.890
I think this project--

00:49:45.890 --> 00:49:47.350
I don't know if it's still--

00:49:47.350 --> 00:49:51.020
I guess it's no
longer exists anymore.

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:54.590
Around 2023, all these sites
like Stack Exchange and Reddit

00:49:54.590 --> 00:49:58.550
realized, wait, people are just
taking our data and training

00:49:58.550 --> 00:50:00.480
models and making
money off of it.

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:05.750
So I think that came to a stop.

00:50:05.750 --> 00:50:10.200
So we have 40 million academic
papers from Semantic Scholar,

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:13.170
which crawls a bunch
of different sites,

00:50:13.170 --> 00:50:15.930
and then we have
our usual suspects.

00:50:15.930 --> 00:50:20.910
So the Common Crawl
processing, which is fairly,

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:23.810
I think I would say standard.

00:50:23.810 --> 00:50:26.660
So they use language
identification

00:50:26.660 --> 00:50:31.050
to keep only the English
part quality filtering.

00:50:31.050 --> 00:50:35.910
Again, in Dolma, for
training an initial model,

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:38.630
they avoided
model-based filtering.

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:41.820
And then toxicity
filtering, they hear you,

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:43.730
they use a classifier.

00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:46.750
And then they do deduplication.

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:50.850
So 3 trillion tokens
came out of that.

00:50:50.850 --> 00:50:54.010
And then in the same year--
so this was last year.

00:50:54.010 --> 00:50:58.950
So there was a
paper DataComp which

00:50:58.950 --> 00:51:01.080
was a collaboration
from multiple different

00:51:01.080 --> 00:51:03.630
organizations.

00:51:03.630 --> 00:51:06.660
Here, what they wanted
to do is foremost

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:13.200
define essentially a competition
for creating data sets.

00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:16.470
And so they wanted to set
up basic infrastructure.

00:51:16.470 --> 00:51:18.452
So they define a
standard data set,

00:51:18.452 --> 00:51:20.910
which you can essentially try
out different data processing

00:51:20.910 --> 00:51:22.710
algorithms.

00:51:22.710 --> 00:51:26.250
So they processed Common
Crawl, all the dumps

00:51:26.250 --> 00:51:30.130
to produce DCLM-pool, which
has 240 trillion tokens.

00:51:30.130 --> 00:51:31.570
So that's a lot of tokens.

00:51:31.570 --> 00:51:34.920
But as you know,
Common Crawl is not

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:36.820
the highest quality on average.

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:40.530
So that's going to get
filtered down quite a bit.

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:46.630
They had a particular recipe for
filtering down that data set,

00:51:46.630 --> 00:51:50.390
which is called DCLM-pool
into DCLM-baseline.

00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:53.430
And here, they were very
aggressive in using a quality

00:51:53.430 --> 00:51:53.930
filter.

00:51:53.930 --> 00:51:55.220
So this is what it looks like.

00:51:55.220 --> 00:51:58.990
They do some rule-based
filtering, basic stuff.

00:51:58.990 --> 00:52:01.210
The main thing
that's interesting

00:52:01.210 --> 00:52:08.890
is that they took this fastText
filter that filtered DCLM-pool

00:52:08.890 --> 00:52:11.330
into DCLM-baseline.

00:52:11.330 --> 00:52:19.180
So they only kept, I guess,
1.4% of the total data set.

00:52:19.180 --> 00:52:22.460
So what do they do for
this model based filtering?

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:24.198
So again, in quality
filtering, you

00:52:24.198 --> 00:52:26.240
define your positive
examples, negative examples,

00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:27.365
and you train a classifier.

00:52:27.365 --> 00:52:30.680
So the positive examples
come from two sources.

00:52:30.680 --> 00:52:34.430
There's this
OpenHermes data set.

00:52:34.430 --> 00:52:37.270
So this is mostly
GPT-4-generated instruction

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:38.150
data.

00:52:38.150 --> 00:52:39.155
So this is interesting.

00:52:39.155 --> 00:52:40.780
They're using actually
instruction data

00:52:40.780 --> 00:52:42.890
to curate pre-training data.

00:52:42.890 --> 00:52:45.530
So now, they're not explicitly
in training on instruction data,

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:47.320
but they're looking
for data that

00:52:47.320 --> 00:52:49.390
looks like instruction data.

00:52:49.390 --> 00:52:58.810
And then ELI5, which is
basically the subreddit called

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:03.520
ELI5, ask me like I'm 5.

00:53:03.520 --> 00:53:07.350
And this is what I guess
the data set looked like.

00:53:09.940 --> 00:53:12.440
What's the point of wasting the
first two plays with a rush?

00:53:15.128 --> 00:53:22.690
So these are questions you might
ask to a chatbot, for example.

00:53:22.690 --> 00:53:27.040
Negative examples are just
sampled from RefinedWeb.

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:31.690
It's not low-quality
data, but it's not

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:35.470
as curated as these
other two sources.

00:53:35.470 --> 00:53:40.580
So the result is that
they took DCLM-baseline,

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:43.670
which is 240 trillion
tokens, and reduced it

00:53:43.670 --> 00:53:49.110
to 3.8 trillion tokens, which
is still a good sizable chunk.

00:53:49.110 --> 00:53:55.370
So then they trained a
fastText classifier on these

00:53:55.370 --> 00:53:58.250
and run it on all of DCLM-pool.

00:53:58.250 --> 00:54:02.480
And here's one of
the results tables.

00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:05.450
So the benchmarks
here are core includes

00:54:05.450 --> 00:54:09.440
a bunch standard language
modeling benchmarks

00:54:09.440 --> 00:54:11.810
like HellaSwag and so on.

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:16.140
And they show that
using this classifier,

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:19.550
they actually outperform
the RefinedWeb

00:54:19.550 --> 00:54:25.800
by 3% and a bunch of
other things by 1% or 2%.

00:54:25.800 --> 00:54:29.540
So this is the
procedure they used

00:54:29.540 --> 00:54:35.300
to create this DCLM-baseline,
which then you train a pretty

00:54:35.300 --> 00:54:38.390
reasonable model from there.

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:41.140
It is worth noting that
after this happened,

00:54:41.140 --> 00:54:44.620
the second OLMo model,
if you remember,

00:54:44.620 --> 00:54:49.990
they started training on
DCLM-baseline as well.

00:54:49.990 --> 00:54:52.050
So I think this
era of we're going

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:55.090
to be unbiased and try to
not use models to bias,

00:54:55.090 --> 00:54:56.700
I think has kind of
largely gone away

00:54:56.700 --> 00:54:59.320
because I think
people realize that,

00:54:59.320 --> 00:55:01.120
well, if you use
models in the loop,

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:02.940
you can just do
a much better job

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:08.550
of getting high-quality
data at least for increasing

00:55:08.550 --> 00:55:11.130
benchmark scores.

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:16.170
So the final
pre-training data set

00:55:16.170 --> 00:55:18.960
I'll talk about is Nemotron-CC.

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:21.840
So this came out
of NVIDIA, which

00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:24.510
has been doing some work on
training the Nemotron models,

00:55:24.510 --> 00:55:26.010
although more
recently, they've been

00:55:26.010 --> 00:55:29.770
doing post-training
and data curation.

00:55:29.770 --> 00:55:36.390
So their main thesis is
that DCLM, the baseline

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:39.790
is great data set, but it
filters very aggressively.

00:55:39.790 --> 00:55:44.770
Remember, it filtered out all
the way from 240 trillion tokens

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:47.620
to 3.8 trillion tokens.

00:55:47.620 --> 00:55:56.960
And if you want to train
larger models for longer,

00:55:56.960 --> 00:56:01.010
you need more tokens because
this aggressive filtering 3.8

00:56:01.010 --> 00:56:04.960
trillion isn't really enough
to sustain, let's say,

00:56:04.960 --> 00:56:07.810
a 400-billion
model training run.

00:56:07.810 --> 00:56:10.690
So the first thing
interesting they realized

00:56:10.690 --> 00:56:14.230
is that they did
ablations for HTML

00:56:14.230 --> 00:56:17.710
to text, but not just
based on the quality

00:56:17.710 --> 00:56:20.600
but on how many
tokens were left.

00:56:20.600 --> 00:56:23.150
So they're really trying to
get not throw away tokens.

00:56:23.150 --> 00:56:26.110
And it turned out that jusText
rather than Trafilatura

00:56:26.110 --> 00:56:27.770
would actually keep more tokens.

00:56:27.770 --> 00:56:30.310
So they went with jusText.

00:56:30.310 --> 00:56:33.250
And then they used a bunch
of different techniques

00:56:33.250 --> 00:56:36.520
to do quality filtering.

00:56:36.520 --> 00:56:39.100
They prompted the
gigantic Nemotron model

00:56:39.100 --> 00:56:42.980
to essentially score documents
based on educational value,

00:56:42.980 --> 00:56:46.490
distilled it into a faster
model, and use that to train.

00:56:46.490 --> 00:56:51.370
So this is a filter based on
what a language model thinks

00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:52.940
is educational value.

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:55.130
And they also use
the DCLM classifier.

00:56:55.130 --> 00:56:59.900
There's an interesting
way that they ensembled.

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:02.080
They basically ran
all these classifiers

00:57:02.080 --> 00:57:04.340
and bucketed their scores.

00:57:04.340 --> 00:57:08.210
And then from each bucket, they
sampled a bunch of data set.

00:57:08.210 --> 00:57:10.390
So it's not just taking
the top, because I

00:57:10.390 --> 00:57:12.520
think they're trying
to make sure they have

00:57:12.520 --> 00:57:19.030
good coverage over different
experts kind of opinion

00:57:19.030 --> 00:57:21.550
on the notion of quality.

00:57:21.550 --> 00:57:24.260
They also did this thing,
which is interesting.

00:57:24.260 --> 00:57:27.070
They used a language model
to not just filter but also

00:57:27.070 --> 00:57:29.030
rephrase data sets.

00:57:29.030 --> 00:57:32.050
So for high-quality data sets--

00:57:32.050 --> 00:57:33.380
sorry, this is backwards.

00:57:33.380 --> 00:57:35.890
For low-quality data
sets, they basically

00:57:35.890 --> 00:57:39.140
use a language model to
rewrite it into something

00:57:39.140 --> 00:57:41.660
that looks higher quality.

00:57:41.660 --> 00:57:43.740
So obviously, there
could be mistakes there.

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:45.480
But in the grand
scheme of things,

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:49.550
maybe it's no worse than
training on low-quality internet

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:50.250
data.

00:57:50.250 --> 00:57:53.450
And for high-quality
data sets, they

00:57:53.450 --> 00:57:56.660
use the language
model to generate

00:57:56.660 --> 00:57:58.170
things that look like tasks.

00:57:58.170 --> 00:58:00.270
So you take a Wikipedia article.

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.318
You ask a language
model to create--

00:58:02.318 --> 00:58:03.860
essentially input
output pairs, where

00:58:03.860 --> 00:58:06.300
the input might be a question
and outputs an answer,

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:08.810
or the input might be
summarize this document

00:58:08.810 --> 00:58:11.090
and outputs a
summary, or the input

00:58:11.090 --> 00:58:13.400
is extract the key
information and the output

00:58:13.400 --> 00:58:14.610
is key information.

00:58:14.610 --> 00:58:19.130
So this is, again, trying
to get at the idea of, well,

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:23.570
eventually an
instruction to any time

00:58:23.570 --> 00:58:27.510
we want to be able
follow instructions.

00:58:27.510 --> 00:58:31.100
So we might as well get
a head start on this.

00:58:31.100 --> 00:58:34.400
So they got 6.3
trillion tokens out

00:58:34.400 --> 00:58:39.660
of that, which is more than 3
trillion tokens, almost double,

00:58:39.660 --> 00:58:42.240
which is I guess, pretty good
because I mean, all of this

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:44.910
is coming from Common Crawl, but
they were able to essentially

00:58:44.910 --> 00:58:46.380
double the--

00:58:46.380 --> 00:58:49.890
maybe not quite double,
but almost double the size.

00:58:49.890 --> 00:58:53.850
For reference, Llama 3 is
trained on 15 trillion.

00:58:53.850 --> 00:58:56.950
Qwen3 is trained on 36 trillion,
which includes, I think,

00:58:56.950 --> 00:58:57.820
multimodal data.

00:58:57.820 --> 00:59:02.290
So 6.3 trillion at this
point is not enormous.

00:59:02.290 --> 00:59:09.990
But for open-source models with
data sets, it's pretty decent.

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:15.030
Most of us, 6.3 trillion is
more than enough for training,

00:59:15.030 --> 00:59:17.520
even to do one epoch.

00:59:17.520 --> 00:59:22.180
This table shows that
basically, on average,

00:59:22.180 --> 00:59:25.080
they show that their
Nemotron-CC data

00:59:25.080 --> 00:59:27.480
is better than the
DCLM data, which

00:59:27.480 --> 00:59:32.640
has been shown to be better than
FineWeb, at least on benchmarks.

00:59:32.640 --> 00:59:35.320
And then they actually
have this 1 trillion

00:59:35.320 --> 00:59:38.085
high-quality subset
that's even better.

00:59:41.510 --> 00:59:43.550
So any questions
before I move on?

00:59:43.550 --> 00:59:46.450
I know this was a lot of
random specific details

00:59:46.450 --> 00:59:50.060
about different
models or data sets,

00:59:50.060 --> 00:59:53.150
but hopefully, this gives you
a sense of the type of things.

00:59:53.150 --> 00:59:56.260
And hopefully, you can
see different patterns,

00:59:56.260 --> 00:59:59.840
whether to use models,
to filter or not,

00:59:59.840 --> 01:00:06.430
whether you're using links
out of high-quality pages

01:00:06.430 --> 01:00:10.030
or using the pages
themselves, and so on.

01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:12.550
So yeah.

01:00:12.550 --> 01:00:14.240
Let's talk about
English data sets.

01:00:14.240 --> 01:00:16.480
I was wondering if
you come across things

01:00:16.480 --> 01:00:19.030
like multilingual data
sets, that kind of thing.

01:00:19.030 --> 01:00:19.880
Yeah, good point.

01:00:19.880 --> 01:00:22.660
So the question is, what
about multilingual data sets?

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:24.940
I've focused on
English because that's

01:00:24.940 --> 01:00:28.970
where primarily a lot
of the research is done.

01:00:28.970 --> 01:00:31.840
But obviously, Common Crawl
does have multilingual data.

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:37.660
And there's multilingual data
sets that are produced as well.

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:41.060
OK, maybe interest of time,
I'll move on to copyright.

01:00:41.060 --> 01:00:43.810
So this was an early
question that was asked,

01:00:43.810 --> 01:00:45.470
how much of the
web is copyrighted?

01:00:45.470 --> 01:00:49.460
So let's just understand what
copyright is really about.

01:00:49.460 --> 01:00:53.410
So nowadays, there's a lot of
lawsuits around generative AI

01:00:53.410 --> 01:00:56.080
mostly around copyright.

01:00:56.080 --> 01:00:59.980
So in general, the copyright
law falls under kind

01:00:59.980 --> 01:01:01.490
of intellectual property law.

01:01:01.490 --> 01:01:04.780
And the goal here is to
incentivize the creation

01:01:04.780 --> 01:01:06.140
of intellectual goods.

01:01:06.140 --> 01:01:10.330
That's why copyright
law even exists.

01:01:10.330 --> 01:01:13.490
And there's many types
of copyrights, patents,

01:01:13.490 --> 01:01:16.540
trade marks, trade secrets.

01:01:16.540 --> 01:01:19.120
So copyright law
is the one that has

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:22.670
been-- is most relevant
for training data.

01:01:22.670 --> 01:01:27.070
And this goes back to
the 1700s in England.

01:01:27.070 --> 01:01:30.660
But in the US,
since 1976, there's

01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:33.780
been the Copyright
Act, which has--

01:01:33.780 --> 01:01:38.580
essentially, it's established
what copyright means.

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:40.590
Formally, it's applied
to original works

01:01:40.590 --> 01:01:45.030
of authorship fixed in a
tangible medium of expression.

01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:48.220
And so it's original work.

01:01:48.220 --> 01:01:51.840
So if you are just a collection,
it's not copyrightable.

01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:54.390
Like telephone directories
are not copyrightable

01:01:54.390 --> 01:01:56.970
unless there's some
creativity in their selection

01:01:56.970 --> 01:01:59.070
or arrangement.

01:01:59.070 --> 01:02:03.280
So copyright also applies to
just expression, not idea.

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:06.250
So you can't copyright
an algorithm.

01:02:06.250 --> 01:02:08.630
You can copyright the code.

01:02:11.220 --> 01:02:14.520
And one thing that has been--

01:02:14.520 --> 01:02:16.980
what this did is that--

01:02:16.980 --> 01:02:20.040
before, copyright
only applied to things

01:02:20.040 --> 01:02:21.940
that had been published.

01:02:21.940 --> 01:02:24.030
And now, it's just this
looser notion of fixed.

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:31.080
In general, copyright
has increased in scope.

01:02:31.080 --> 01:02:35.913
And registration is not
required for copyright.

01:02:35.913 --> 01:02:37.330
So this is different
from patents.

01:02:37.330 --> 01:02:39.288
If you invent something
and you don't register,

01:02:39.288 --> 01:02:40.630
then you have no claim to it.

01:02:40.630 --> 01:02:43.350
Whereas copyright
is you put something

01:02:43.350 --> 01:02:45.790
and you throw it
up on your website,

01:02:45.790 --> 01:02:49.230
it's copyrighted even
if you don't explicitly

01:02:49.230 --> 01:02:51.622
write copyrighted.

01:02:54.300 --> 01:02:57.600
But there is a thing,
that registration

01:02:57.600 --> 01:03:01.140
is required before a
creator can sue someone

01:03:01.140 --> 01:03:02.830
for copyright infringement.

01:03:02.830 --> 01:03:06.760
But the bar for registration
is also only $65,

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:10.320
as opposed to patents,
which can be thousands.

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:13.620
It now lasts 75 years,
and then the copyright

01:03:13.620 --> 01:03:16.090
expires and becomes part
of the public domain.

01:03:16.090 --> 01:03:20.070
So all the classics and
most of Project Gutenberg

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:23.070
has been gone out of copyright.

01:03:23.070 --> 01:03:26.272
So the thing that maybe
people might not realize

01:03:26.272 --> 01:03:27.730
is that most things
on the internet

01:03:27.730 --> 01:03:29.360
are actually copyrighted.

01:03:29.360 --> 01:03:32.330
So whether something copyrighted
isn't really the issue.

01:03:32.330 --> 01:03:35.030
So the issue is
whether you can use it.

01:03:35.030 --> 01:03:37.910
And there's two
ways you can use it.

01:03:37.910 --> 01:03:41.680
You can either get a license for
it or you appeal to the fair use

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:42.560
clause.

01:03:42.560 --> 01:03:45.940
So if you're going
the license route,

01:03:45.940 --> 01:03:49.870
you can either go
sign a contract

01:03:49.870 --> 01:03:53.650
with the creator
to get a license

01:03:53.650 --> 01:03:56.150
to use the data in some terms.

01:03:56.150 --> 01:03:58.450
And this is essentially
what does with--

01:03:58.450 --> 01:04:02.020
for example, Google and
Reddit have this relationship.

01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:04.110
And effectively,
license is don't sue me.

01:04:06.640 --> 01:04:08.560
There's a special
type of license

01:04:08.560 --> 01:04:11.770
called the Creative
Commons license, which

01:04:11.770 --> 01:04:14.300
allows the free distribution
of copyrighted work.

01:04:14.300 --> 01:04:17.660
So Creative Commons, all that
stuff is still copyrighted.

01:04:17.660 --> 01:04:19.300
It's just that you
have a license that

01:04:19.300 --> 01:04:23.270
enables it to act like if
it was in the public domain.

01:04:23.270 --> 01:04:26.170
So a lot of things are--

01:04:26.170 --> 01:04:30.560
like Wikipedia, for example, is
all Creative Commons license.

01:04:30.560 --> 01:04:33.210
And a lot of YouTube videos
are also Creative Commons.

01:04:33.210 --> 01:04:37.380
And this was created almost,
I guess, 20 years ago,

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:39.680
to essentially bridge the
gap between public domain

01:04:39.680 --> 01:04:41.660
and copyright.

01:04:41.660 --> 01:04:44.540
And the idea is
that you want people

01:04:44.540 --> 01:04:49.460
to be able to use this stuff
without waiting 75 years.

01:04:49.460 --> 01:04:53.180
And there are cases where
the creator is actually

01:04:53.180 --> 01:04:58.970
happy for people to use their
content, but most of the time,

01:04:58.970 --> 01:05:03.770
it's just not clear because
they haven't said yes or no.

01:05:03.770 --> 01:05:07.070
So now, many model developers
license data for training

01:05:07.070 --> 01:05:10.430
foundation models, for example,
Google and Reddit, OpenAI

01:05:10.430 --> 01:05:14.370
and Shutterstock, OpenAI and
Stack Exchange, and so on.

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:20.090
So if you have money,
you go get a license.

01:05:20.090 --> 01:05:22.820
If you don't, then
I guess you have

01:05:22.820 --> 01:05:26.180
to say you're a poor academic,
and then maybe they'll

01:05:26.180 --> 01:05:26.980
let you use it.

01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:31.880
But the problem
is that you can't

01:05:31.880 --> 01:05:35.270
get a license to the internet.

01:05:35.270 --> 01:05:36.410
You go to a random website.

01:05:36.410 --> 01:05:38.400
Who do you even go talk to?

01:05:38.400 --> 01:05:41.720
So the only way you
can use it legally

01:05:41.720 --> 01:05:43.980
is to appeal to fair use.

01:05:43.980 --> 01:05:49.378
So fair use says basically,
even if something is copyright

01:05:49.378 --> 01:05:51.170
and you don't have a
license, you can still

01:05:51.170 --> 01:05:53.370
use it under some conditions.

01:05:53.370 --> 01:05:57.200
And the conditions are
determined by the purpose

01:05:57.200 --> 01:05:58.380
and character of the use.

01:05:58.380 --> 01:05:59.797
So for example,
if you're using it

01:05:59.797 --> 01:06:02.670
for educational rather
than commercial,

01:06:02.670 --> 01:06:05.330
or you're transforming
the work in some way

01:06:05.330 --> 01:06:08.060
rather than just like copying it
and hosting it on your website

01:06:08.060 --> 01:06:13.100
and pretending it's your own,
that's going to help you.

01:06:13.100 --> 01:06:21.380
What the work is,
if it's fictional,

01:06:21.380 --> 01:06:24.120
it's more likely to be--

01:06:24.120 --> 01:06:25.800
actually, if it's,
sorry, factual, it's

01:06:25.800 --> 01:06:28.680
more likely to be fair use.

01:06:28.680 --> 01:06:30.130
For example, the telephone book.

01:06:30.130 --> 01:06:34.505
You can't really copyright
things that are closer to facts.

01:06:37.320 --> 01:06:39.060
If you use this just
a snippet, then it's

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:41.158
more likely to be
copyright fair use.

01:06:41.158 --> 01:06:43.200
Although for language
models, that doesn't really

01:06:43.200 --> 01:06:45.450
apply because you probably
want to train on all of it,

01:06:45.450 --> 01:06:47.070
not just a snippet.

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:48.940
And then effect on the market.

01:06:48.940 --> 01:06:53.100
So for example, if you're using
the work to essentially displace

01:06:53.100 --> 01:06:56.310
the creator, then that's going
to be seen less favorably

01:06:56.310 --> 01:06:58.920
than if you're using the work
to do something completely

01:06:58.920 --> 01:06:59.740
different.

01:06:59.740 --> 01:07:03.460
So if you obviously watch a
movie and write a summary of it,

01:07:03.460 --> 01:07:04.330
it's fair use.

01:07:04.330 --> 01:07:07.020
If you reimplement
the idea, that's fine.

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:10.110
There's a big,
long-decade fight over

01:07:10.110 --> 01:07:12.460
whether Google Books
when they show snippets,

01:07:12.460 --> 01:07:14.620
whether that's fair use or not.

01:07:14.620 --> 01:07:19.650
And eventually, it ruled
in favor of Google.

01:07:19.650 --> 01:07:23.060
It's also worth noting
that copyright isn't just

01:07:23.060 --> 01:07:25.440
about verbatim memorization.

01:07:25.440 --> 01:07:28.950
So it turns out that plots and
characters can be copyrightable.

01:07:28.950 --> 01:07:34.260
So even if you have essentially
very little n-gram overlap,

01:07:34.260 --> 01:07:36.380
but you take Harry
Potter, the character,

01:07:36.380 --> 01:07:39.260
and you essentially
develop it, then

01:07:39.260 --> 01:07:43.650
that could be a
violation of copyright.

01:07:43.650 --> 01:07:47.220
But on the other hand, if you
parity, it might be fair use.

01:07:47.220 --> 01:07:50.210
So these things
are quite subtle.

01:07:50.210 --> 01:07:52.760
Copyright is all about the
semantics and the economics

01:07:52.760 --> 01:07:54.600
and the kind of content.

01:07:54.600 --> 01:07:59.000
So it's a very
complicated topic.

01:07:59.000 --> 01:08:01.340
So what about for training?

01:08:01.340 --> 01:08:07.940
So one thing is
that copyright is--

01:08:07.940 --> 01:08:10.243
copy is in the name copyright.

01:08:10.243 --> 01:08:11.660
The first step of
training, you're

01:08:11.660 --> 01:08:16.319
copying the data is technically
already a violation,

01:08:16.319 --> 01:08:20.330
even if you don't
do anything with it.

01:08:20.330 --> 01:08:25.399
You could argue, as many
have, that training ML model

01:08:25.399 --> 01:08:29.120
is transformative
because it's definitely

01:08:29.120 --> 01:08:32.630
far from just copy and pasting.

01:08:32.630 --> 01:08:37.069
This does make, I think,
open-source models

01:08:37.069 --> 01:08:40.033
with open data bit
challenging, because if you

01:08:40.033 --> 01:08:41.450
want to train a
model and you also

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:44.250
want to show people your
data and you host that data,

01:08:44.250 --> 01:08:47.660
that could be in
violation of copyright.

01:08:47.660 --> 01:08:53.240
People have also made arguments
that the machine learning

01:08:53.240 --> 01:08:56.297
system is interested in the
idea, not the expression.

01:08:56.297 --> 01:08:58.130
You're training all
this data because you're

01:08:58.130 --> 01:09:01.399
trying to extract
how language works

01:09:01.399 --> 01:09:04.700
and general knowledge,
rather than interest

01:09:04.700 --> 01:09:06.120
in a particular work.

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:09.340
But of course, the learning
algorithm has been--

01:09:09.340 --> 01:09:09.840
sorry.

01:09:09.840 --> 01:09:11.689
The models can often
memorize, and you

01:09:11.689 --> 01:09:17.960
can extract training data
from the models quite easily.

01:09:17.960 --> 01:09:22.430
And there's also this
problem that language models

01:09:22.430 --> 01:09:27.770
can definitely affect the
market regardless of copyright.

01:09:27.770 --> 01:09:30.710
One, I guess,
other thing to note

01:09:30.710 --> 01:09:34.250
is that even if you have a
license and if you can appeal

01:09:34.250 --> 01:09:36.210
to fair use for a
particular work,

01:09:36.210 --> 01:09:39.170
you still might not be able to
legally get the data because

01:09:39.170 --> 01:09:40.080
of terms of use.

01:09:40.080 --> 01:09:45.330
For example, YouTube has a lot
of Creative Commons videos,

01:09:45.330 --> 01:09:47.840
but if you write a script
that goes and downloads

01:09:47.840 --> 01:09:51.220
your videos from YouTube, that
is against the YouTube's term

01:09:51.220 --> 01:09:51.720
of service.

01:09:51.720 --> 01:09:55.340
So there's another gating
for these platforms.

01:09:55.340 --> 01:09:59.515
There's a bunch of works that
you can read about later.

01:10:02.270 --> 01:10:06.240
OK, so let me quickly go
on in the interest of time.

01:10:06.240 --> 01:10:10.100
So this section is going
to be a bit shorter.

01:10:10.100 --> 01:10:13.490
And I've collapsed mid-training
and post-training together

01:10:13.490 --> 01:10:18.330
because the boundary is
not often quite clear.

01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:21.850
Often now, we're thinking about
less high quality in general,

01:10:21.850 --> 01:10:25.480
but focused on how do you
instill particular capabilities,

01:10:25.480 --> 01:10:28.770
although even in
pre-training, we were already

01:10:28.770 --> 01:10:31.870
thinking about quality
classifiers and high quality.

01:10:31.870 --> 01:10:35.520
So again, the line
is not quite clear.

01:10:35.520 --> 01:10:38.430
So one thing that I think is--

01:10:38.430 --> 01:10:40.750
which we haven't really
talked about in this class,

01:10:40.750 --> 01:10:42.580
is long context.

01:10:42.580 --> 01:10:45.750
So models, if you look
at the top models,

01:10:45.750 --> 01:10:47.320
have quite a bit of context.

01:10:47.320 --> 01:10:49.480
Gemini I think still has--

01:10:49.480 --> 01:10:54.310
I think Llama 4 might advertise
a 10 million contexts,

01:10:54.310 --> 01:10:56.610
but the context lengths
are quite large.

01:10:56.610 --> 01:11:01.878
And transformers
scale quadratically

01:11:01.878 --> 01:11:02.920
with the sequence length.

01:11:02.920 --> 01:11:04.930
I mean, we saw the
inference lecture,

01:11:04.930 --> 01:11:07.200
you can get around that,
but still you need,

01:11:07.200 --> 01:11:11.980
I think, full attention
to get the best results.

01:11:11.980 --> 01:11:14.940
And clearly, you don't want
to start at the beginning

01:11:14.940 --> 01:11:16.780
training on long context.

01:11:16.780 --> 01:11:18.710
So what people do is
they add it later.

01:11:18.710 --> 01:11:21.250
So that's why long context
extension often shows up

01:11:21.250 --> 01:11:23.500
at mid-training
because you don't

01:11:23.500 --> 01:11:27.010
want to waste cycles
training on long contexts

01:11:27.010 --> 01:11:29.740
if your model is not very good.

01:11:29.740 --> 01:11:32.980
So there's multiple
ways of doing that.

01:11:32.980 --> 01:11:34.580
But since this is
a data lecture,

01:11:34.580 --> 01:11:41.830
I'll talk about books
and math as our two

01:11:41.830 --> 01:11:44.772
sources that have been used
to do context extension.

01:11:44.772 --> 01:11:46.480
Basically for context
attention, you just

01:11:46.480 --> 01:11:51.080
need to create data that
has long range dependencies.

01:11:51.080 --> 01:11:55.660
And some of this data
can also be synthesized.

01:11:55.660 --> 01:12:00.950
So people also look at tasks.

01:12:00.950 --> 01:12:06.770
So there's a bunch of works
that essentially convert

01:12:06.770 --> 01:12:11.380
traditional NLP benchmarks
into a standard format

01:12:11.380 --> 01:12:13.490
that they can be fine tuned on.

01:12:13.490 --> 01:12:18.530
So supernatural instructions
is one such data sets that

01:12:18.530 --> 01:12:27.320
leveraged the community to come
together and create 1,600 tasks,

01:12:27.320 --> 01:12:31.250
and they standardize
into a prompt.

01:12:31.250 --> 01:12:34.760
Flan was around the same year.

01:12:34.760 --> 01:12:38.040
It came out in 2022, but
the paper was in 2023.

01:12:38.040 --> 01:12:42.020
So 2022 was the year of
let's take all the NLP tasks

01:12:42.020 --> 01:12:45.760
and shove them into
instruction-following format.

01:12:52.220 --> 01:12:54.380
One, I think, advantage
of this is now you

01:12:54.380 --> 01:12:57.560
have a language model that can
solve all your favorite NLP

01:12:57.560 --> 01:12:59.790
tasks, and you benefit
from transfer learning.

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:03.050
This is a lot of
thinking about--

01:13:03.050 --> 01:13:05.600
going back to T5.

01:13:05.600 --> 01:13:10.490
But one problem with
this is that often, the

01:13:10.490 --> 01:13:14.150
prompts that you have
are very templatized.

01:13:14.150 --> 01:13:17.750
If you look at the supernatural
instructions, some of it

01:13:17.750 --> 01:13:22.280
is not supernatural
because they're

01:13:22.280 --> 01:13:24.660
all look kind of the same.

01:13:24.660 --> 01:13:30.300
So that motivates these
instruction-following data sets.

01:13:30.300 --> 01:13:34.670
And since 2022, there's
been this expectation

01:13:34.670 --> 01:13:36.500
that language models
should just be

01:13:36.500 --> 01:13:40.410
able to answer any one-off
task that you give it.

01:13:40.410 --> 01:13:43.730
So the notion of even
tasks disappears.

01:13:43.730 --> 01:13:46.670
So a lot of the work
in the open community

01:13:46.670 --> 01:13:49.110
has been based on
synthetic data,

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:54.980
starting with Alpaca, which
used this idea of self-instruct

01:13:54.980 --> 01:13:58.340
to prompt a language model
to generate examples,

01:13:58.340 --> 01:14:02.120
which you can then
use for fine tuning.

01:14:02.120 --> 01:14:05.480
There's Vicuna, which used
these conversations that

01:14:05.480 --> 01:14:08.630
had been shared by
users on ShareGPT, which

01:14:08.630 --> 01:14:10.250
is deprecated now.

01:14:10.250 --> 01:14:14.380
You can get language models
to chat with themselves,

01:14:14.380 --> 01:14:16.770
seeded with some
questions, and that

01:14:16.770 --> 01:14:21.030
creates some synthetic data.

01:14:21.030 --> 01:14:27.570
And you can also have
these evol-instruct methods

01:14:27.570 --> 01:14:33.030
that essentially take questions
and make them more complicated.

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:37.590
There's other ways of taking--

01:14:37.590 --> 01:14:41.310
this one takes
Common Crawl and then

01:14:41.310 --> 01:14:45.160
uses it to essentially look at--

01:14:45.160 --> 01:14:47.910
identify quiz sites and
then extract QA pairs

01:14:47.910 --> 01:14:50.580
using a language model.

01:14:50.580 --> 01:14:53.640
And then this is an OpenHermes,
which we saw earlier

01:14:53.640 --> 01:14:54.960
from the DCLM work.

01:14:54.960 --> 01:14:59.070
It's just an agglomeration
of different data sets.

01:14:59.070 --> 01:15:01.900
Llama 2 chat, we don't know
what the exact data set is,

01:15:01.900 --> 01:15:06.840
but they used annotators
to essentially write

01:15:06.840 --> 01:15:09.940
high-quality instruction data.

01:15:09.940 --> 01:15:15.100
And they claim in
this paper that this

01:15:15.100 --> 01:15:17.050
was better than using
the millions of examples

01:15:17.050 --> 01:15:18.120
from open data sets.

01:15:22.450 --> 01:15:26.620
But they could have
even saved more money

01:15:26.620 --> 01:15:34.610
by less annotation
and more just RLHF,

01:15:34.610 --> 01:15:37.120
which we'll talk about later.

01:15:37.120 --> 01:15:40.180
And finally, the last stage
that I'll mention just

01:15:40.180 --> 01:15:45.970
came out pretty recently, so
the Llama-Nemotron post-training

01:15:45.970 --> 01:15:47.080
data.

01:15:47.080 --> 01:15:50.337
This consists of a
bunch of-- there aren't

01:15:50.337 --> 01:15:51.920
that many details
about this data set,

01:15:51.920 --> 01:15:55.240
but the data set released,
so you can go and look at it.

01:15:55.240 --> 01:15:58.850
They have public data
sets like WildChat.

01:15:58.850 --> 01:16:01.480
And then they synthetically
generated some data

01:16:01.480 --> 01:16:07.490
from all the models that you're
able to generate data from.

01:16:07.490 --> 01:16:10.915
They also include reasoning
traces, thanks to R1.

01:16:13.670 --> 01:16:21.530
So if you look at this
data, you can divide it

01:16:21.530 --> 01:16:23.150
into a few buckets.

01:16:23.150 --> 01:16:26.610
One is that a lot of the
early work was just, OK,

01:16:26.610 --> 01:16:28.070
there's GPT-4.

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:32.910
This is the easiest way to
generate synthetic data.

01:16:32.910 --> 01:16:35.150
The problem with that is that--

01:16:35.150 --> 01:16:36.900
for academic
research, it's fine,

01:16:36.900 --> 01:16:39.920
but it is against
the terms of OpenAI

01:16:39.920 --> 01:16:44.480
to use GPT-4 to create a data
set that you train a competing

01:16:44.480 --> 01:16:46.070
model.

01:16:46.070 --> 01:16:52.680
Whereas these open-weight models
have more permissive licenses,

01:16:52.680 --> 01:16:55.280
which means that you can
essentially distill from them

01:16:55.280 --> 01:16:57.480
and do whatever you want.

01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:00.060
There might be some
restriction on Llama,

01:17:00.060 --> 01:17:03.560
but I think broadly
speaking, I think they're

01:17:03.560 --> 01:17:05.450
more permissive than OpenAI.

01:17:05.450 --> 01:17:09.590
And then finally, if you
are really paranoid, then

01:17:09.590 --> 01:17:11.420
you can actually
just hire annotators

01:17:11.420 --> 01:17:14.840
to create high-quality
instruction, which is obviously

01:17:14.840 --> 01:17:16.710
more expensive and slower.

01:17:16.710 --> 01:17:20.720
And there's also this worry
that annotators might actually

01:17:20.720 --> 01:17:23.190
use GPT-4 to create your data.

01:17:23.190 --> 01:17:26.480
So you have to be careful there.

01:17:26.480 --> 01:17:30.740
To summarize, so the
key lesson is data just

01:17:30.740 --> 01:17:32.970
doesn't fall from the sky.

01:17:32.970 --> 01:17:36.620
You have to really
work hard to get it.

01:17:36.620 --> 01:17:39.920
And it's important to think
that out there in the world,

01:17:39.920 --> 01:17:42.930
there's these live
services like GitHub.

01:17:42.930 --> 01:17:46.830
And first, you have to use it.

01:17:46.830 --> 01:17:50.580
You have to first get
a dump of the raw data.

01:17:50.580 --> 01:17:52.230
But you can't train
on the raw data.

01:17:52.230 --> 01:17:54.390
It's too big or it's
too noisy or it has--

01:17:54.390 --> 01:17:58.490
it's not even tokens.

01:17:58.490 --> 01:18:00.270
And you often have
to process it.

01:18:00.270 --> 01:18:02.990
And that's where a
lot of heuristics

01:18:02.990 --> 01:18:06.280
that we saw for quality
filtering and deduplication

01:18:06.280 --> 01:18:08.560
kind of fit in.

01:18:08.560 --> 01:18:10.370
This was touched on earlier.

01:18:10.370 --> 01:18:14.560
Data is really the key
ingredient that essentially

01:18:14.560 --> 01:18:16.970
differentiates language models.

01:18:16.970 --> 01:18:19.820
I think all of the language
model architectures,

01:18:19.820 --> 01:18:22.215
there's some
transformer MOE style.

01:18:25.730 --> 01:18:28.150
These architectures is
so of general purpose

01:18:28.150 --> 01:18:31.190
that the behaviors aren't really
going to be that different.

01:18:31.190 --> 01:18:34.780
It's really the data that drives
the quality there, assuming

01:18:34.780 --> 01:18:38.380
you can train and fit the data.

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:41.780
There are some legal
and ethical issues here.

01:18:41.780 --> 01:18:44.260
We talk about copyright,
but there's much more

01:18:44.260 --> 01:18:45.710
to be said here.

01:18:45.710 --> 01:18:49.728
And then finally, if you think
that this whole field is a mess,

01:18:49.728 --> 01:18:50.270
you're right.

01:18:50.270 --> 01:18:53.920
It's very heuristic, which means
that there's many opportunities

01:18:53.920 --> 01:18:56.440
to hopefully improve that.

01:18:56.440 --> 01:18:58.580
OK, that is it.

01:18:58.580 --> 01:19:01.260
And I'll see you on Thursday.
